{
  "families": [
    {
      "budget_share": 0.55,
      "family": "iterative",
      "ranges": [
        {
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ],
          "high": 4,
          "low": 0,
          "param": "frac_guard"
        },
        {
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ],
          "high": 4,
          "low": -2,
          "param": "angle_guard"
        },
        {
          "choices": null,
          "high": 16,
          "low": 12,
          "param": "n_iter"
        },
        {
          "choices": null,
          "high": 20,
          "low": 14,
          "param": "data_width"
        }
      ],
      "why": "iterative is the smallest-area family in the registry (shared datapath, no per-stage registers), so it is the natural home for the min luts_plus_ffs objective. The throughput constraint (>=1 MSPS) is met by any iterative design whose Fmax is >=1000 MHz, which is impossible; however, the constraint is on throughput_msps = Fmax * results/cycle. For iterative, results/cycle = 1/(N+3). To get >=1 MSPS we need Fmax/(N+3) >= 1 MHz, i.e. Fmax >= (N+3) MHz. For N=12..16, that is 15..19 MHz, which is trivially met. So iterative can meet throughput. Accuracy: max_abs_err <= 2^-10 = 0.000976562. CORDIC error is dominated by the residual after N iterations, roughly 2^-(N+2) for the angle path, plus truncation/rounding effects. With N=12, residual ~2^-14, well below 2^-10. Even N=10 gives ~2^-12. So n_iter 12..16 is a safe band that keeps area low while guaranteeing accuracy. data_width 14..20: output LSB is 2^-(W-2). For W=14, LSB=2^-12, which is below 2^-10, so the quantization alone is fine. Lower W saves area. angle_guard and frac_guard are swept to find the minimum that still meets accuracy, since they add bits to the datapath and cost LUTs."
    },
    {
      "budget_share": 0.45,
      "family": "unrolled_k",
      "ranges": [
        {
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ],
          "high": 4,
          "low": 0,
          "param": "frac_guard"
        },
        {
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ],
          "high": 4,
          "low": -2,
          "param": "angle_guard"
        },
        {
          "choices": null,
          "high": 16,
          "low": 12,
          "param": "n_iter"
        },
        {
          "choices": null,
          "high": 20,
          "low": 14,
          "param": "data_width"
        },
        {
          "choices": null,
          "high": 4,
          "low": 2,
          "param": "k"
        }
      ],
      "why": "unrolled_k with k>1 reduces the cycle count per result (ceil(N/k)+3), which raises throughput for a given Fmax and may allow a lower Fmax to meet the 1 MSPS constraint, potentially freeing up timing margin that translates to lower area (smaller critical path). For k=2..4 and N=12..16, cycles/result = ceil(N/k)+3 ranges from ~6 to 11, so required Fmax is 6..11 MHz, still trivial. The main trade-off is that unrolling k rotations per cycle adds parallel datapath logic (k shifters/adders), increasing LUTs. The search will reveal whether the area penalty of unrolling is worth any Fmax/area interaction. Keeping the same n_iter and data_width ranges as iterative allows a direct comparison of the two families' Pareto fronts. k is limited to 2..4 to avoid excessive area blowup; k=8 would roughly double the datapath width and is unlikely to be area-optimal for this spec."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The spec demands >=1 MSPS throughput and max_abs_err <= 2^-10, with area (luts_plus_ffs) as the primary objective. Both iterative and unrolled_k can meet the throughput constraint easily because the required Fmax is only single-digit to low-double-digit MHz for N=12..16. Accuracy is comfortably met with N>=12 iterations, so the search focuses on the low-N, low-width corner of the space where area is minimized. data_width 14..20 keeps the output quantization (LSB = 2^-(W-2)) at or below 2^-12, safely under the 2^-10 error budget, while allowing the optimizer to find the minimum W that still meets accuracy once angle_guard and frac_guard are accounted for. angle_guard and frac_guard are swept over their full ranges because they directly trade bits (area) against accuracy; the optimizer will find the minimum guard values that keep max_abs_err <= 2^-10. Rounding mode is included because round vs trunc can shift the error by up to 1 LSB, which matters near the accuracy boundary. The budget is split 55/45 between iterative and unrolled_k: iterative is expected to be the area winner (shared datapath, no parallel logic), so it gets more evaluations to finely map its Pareto front. unrolled_k gets 45% to check whether unrolling provides any area benefit through timing relaxation, and to populate the front with alternative points. Together, these two families cover the full area trade-off space available to this fleet member."
}

{
  "decision": "stop",
  "rationale": "The feasible Pareto front is well-established with 7 distinct designs spanning luts_plus_ffs 281-361 and accuracy_bits 11.5-14.6. The minimum area design (iterative, luts_plus_ffs=281) meets all constraints: throughput 10.4 MSPS >= 1 MSPS and max_abs_err 2^-11.50 <= 2^-10. The front covers the relevant trade-off space for this spec, and since this is the final round, we stop and select the minimum-area design from the front."
,
  "next_plan": null
}
